1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:05,540
The third subtype of anemia is normocytic anemia.

2
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:08,540
And recall, based on the paradigm that we've established

3
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:12,500
when we discussed both microcytic and macrocytic anemias,

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,420
that a normocytic anemia will be defined by the fact

5
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:20,060
that there is an anemia, i.e. a decrease in red blood cell mass.

6
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,400
However, the red blood cells are normal-sized.

7
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:27,080
And again, the key measure of size that we've been using

8
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,520
is the MCV, or the mean corpuscular volume.

9
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:33,040
And in a normocytic anemia, the MCV will be normal,

10
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,660
which is between 80 and 100.

11
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,620
Now, in normocytic anemias, the key differential diagnosis

12
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:45,100
is, is the anemia due to peripheral destruction,

13
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,660
i.e., are the red blood cells being destroyed

14
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,360
outside of the bone marrow,

15
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:53,540
or is the anemia due to underproduction,

16
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:57,900
i.e., is the bone marrow not making red blood cells?

17
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,980
And the way we can distinguish between these two etiologies

18
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:04,320
is to look at the reticulocyte count.

19
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:07,620
Reticulocytes are young red blood cells

20
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,900
that are obviously released from the bone marrow.

21
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:12,040
You can think of them as baby red blood cells.

22
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,980
On a blood smear, they're identified as cells

23
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:16,760
that are slightly larger than normal,

24
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:20,060
just a tad bit larger, with bluish cytoplasm.

25
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:23,780
And the bluish cytoplasm is due to residual RNA

26
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:24,920
within the cell.

27
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:27,660
Basically, when the young red blood cell is born,

28
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:29,940
it's born with no nucleus,

29
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,680
and it has most of its hemoglobin.

30
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:36,460
However, there is some residual processing of RNA

31
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:40,100
and protein synthesis that continues to occur

32
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,020
in the first day or so after birth.

33
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:44,360
Reticulocytes look like this.

34
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:45,780
Here's a red blood cell smear,

35
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:48,160
and you can see that there is this one cell

36
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,440
that's just a tad larger than the others

37
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:54,420
and that has this bluish hue to its cytoplasm.

38
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:57,680
And again, the bluish hue is due to the presence of RNA

39
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:58,960
within the cytoplasm.

40
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:01,880
It's important to know why the reticulocyte looks blue.

41
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,340
Now, normally, the reticulocyte count

42
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:07,880
in the peripheral blood smear is about 1 to 2%.

43
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:10,740
Each day, about 1 to 2% of cells

44
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:12,220
are removed from the circulation

45
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:14,320
and replaced by reticulocytes.

46
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:17,340
In a properly functioning bone marrow,

47
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:20,640
the reticulocyte count, if there was an anemia,

48
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:23,200
should pump up to greater than 3%.

49
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,640
What I mean by this is that when there is an anemia,

50
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:28,960
the bone marrow should respond

51
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:30,560
by making more reticulocytes

52
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,780
in order to try to make up for the loss of red blood cells.

53
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:36,120
And the bone marrow does have the ability

54
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,040
to pump up the reticulocyte production

55
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,140
so that the reticulocyte count

56
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:43,260
in the peripheral blood smear would be greater than 3%.

57
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:46,300
However, we also need to keep in mind

58
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,660
that with an anemia,

59
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:50,160
the reticulocyte count will be falsely elevated.

60
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,160
And that's because the reticulocytes

61
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,640
are measured as a percentage

62
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,080
of the total red blood cells.

63
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:59,420
Let's assume that a patient has 98 red blood cells

64
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:02,600
and two of those red blood cells are reticulocytes.

65
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,280
So then this would mean that the reticulocyte count

66
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:08,100
in a normal patient is 2 out of 100.

67
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:11,800
98 plus 2 is 100, so 2 out of 100.

68
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,760
And therefore, the reticulocyte count is 2%.

69
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:17,540
Now, let's imagine that the patient gets an anemia.

70
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:19,200
If the patient gets an anemia,

71
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,060
if we assume that 50 of these 98 red blood cells

72
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:25,820
get destroyed or about half of the cells get destroyed,

73
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:28,520
then the patient would have 48 red blood cells.

74
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:31,920
And because the vast majority of what gets destroyed

75
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:34,020
are going to be the normal red blood cells,

76
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:36,000
the patient would have two reticulocytes.

77
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:39,020
And so now the patient has two out of 50.

78
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:43,000
48 plus 2 is 50, 2 over 50, which is really 4 over 100,

79
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:44,400
or 4%.

80
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,740
Now, you see that the reticulocyte count went up to 4%.

81
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:52,780
However, has the bone marrow made any extra red blood cells

82
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:53,520
in this circumstance?

83
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:54,900
The answer is no.

84
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,820
It's still making those same two reticulocytes.

85
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:01,380
And so you can see that in a patient with anemia,

86
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:04,860
the reticulocyte count is falsely elevated.

87
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,240
And that's what I was trying to say here in your text,

88
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:11,860
that a decrease in total red blood cells falsely elevates

89
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:13,700
the percentage of reticulocytes.

90
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:17,820
Therefore, in order to adjust for the anemia,

91
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:20,740
we need to correct the reticulocyte count.

92
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:23,620
And how do we correct the reticulocyte count?

93
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:27,220
By multiplying it by the hematocrit divided by 45.

94
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,360
That then would correct the reticulocyte count.

95
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:31,880
So let's go back to the whiteboard.

96
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:34,940
Remember in our example, the patient normally had 98 cells

97
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:38,620
and two reticulocytes giving a percentage of 2%.

98
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:42,880
And then the patient got an anemia so that half of their red blood cells were destroyed.

99
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:45,480
So roughly 48 red blood cells are present.

100
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:48,360
And then the reticulocyte count remained 2.

101
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:52,480
And we said that that reticulocyte count now becomes 4%, 2 over 50.

102
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:59,420
Well, in essence, this anemia represents a destruction of about half of the red blood cells.

103
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:06,500
And so the way we would correct this 4% is to take the 4% and multiply it by the patient's hematocrit

104
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,600
over the normal hematocrit, which is about 45.

105
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:13,520
So let's say that the patient has lost half of their red blood cells.

106
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:16,220
Their hematocrit would be half of normal, about 23.

107
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:21,320
So 23 over 45 would be approximately 1 half, just roughly.

108
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:23,780
Obviously, 23 over 46 is 1 half.

109
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:28,440
But this would be 1 half times 4%, which would then bring our reticulocyte count,

110
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:31,680
our corrected reticulocyte count, down to 2%.

111
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:35,860
And so by looking at the corrected reticulocyte count,

112
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:41,300
we realize that the bone marrow really is not responding well, in this particular example,

113
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:43,380
to the stress of the anemia.

114
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:46,960
And therefore, we would say that there is a problem with bone marrow function.

115
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:49,760
Now, again, this is a very important discussion

116
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:54,960
because when we have a normal cytokinemia, we have to start by asking ourselves a very simple question.

117
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:58,320
Are the cells being destroyed outside of the bone marrow,

118
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:01,800
or are the cells not being produced by the bone marrow?

119
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,140
And if the corrected reticulocyte count is greater than 3%,

120
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:08,620
we know that the bone marrow is producing red blood cells, fine.

121
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:13,200
But if the corrected reticulocyte count is less than 3%,

122
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:16,440
then we know that there could be a problem with the bone marrow.

123
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:21,080
So correcting the reticulocyte count is a relatively high-yield concept.

124
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:24,680
When we think about destruction of red blood cells in the periphery,

125
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,900
i.e. the bone marrow is making red blood cells fine,

126
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:31,800
but the cells are being destroyed in the periphery, that's called hemolysis.

127
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:37,240
And hemolysis can be divided into extravascular and intravascular hemolysis.

128
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:43,260
In extravascular hemolysis, the red blood cells are destroyed by the reticuloendothelial system,

129
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:46,260
the macrophages of the spleen, the liver, and the lymph nodes,

130
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:49,240
and this occurs outside of the blood vessel.

131
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:54,440
In intravascular hemolysis, the red blood cells are destroyed within the blood vessel.

132
00:06:54,960 --> 00:07:00,500
Now in both cases, we're going to get an anemia because we're destroying red blood cells,

133
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,180
but there will be a good bone marrow response.

134
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:08,420
That is, the bone marrow will be producing greater than 3% reticulocytes

135
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:10,820
if we look at the corrected reticulocyte count.

136
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:18,380
It's important to be aware of the basic principles that underlie each of the types of hemolysis

137
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:21,400
so that we can understand the clinical findings that will be seen

138
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:25,280
if there is extravascular or intravascular hemolysis.

139
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:27,880
So let's start with extravascular hemolysis.

140
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:31,180
Recall that I said that in extravascular hemolysis,

141
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:35,500
the red blood cells are destroyed by the reticuloendothelial system.

142
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:42,400
The reticuloendothelial system is made up of the macrophages of the spleen, liver, and the lymph nodes,

143
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:48,120
and the macrophages, they consume red blood cells and then break down the hemoglobin.

144
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:51,300
When they eat the red blood cells, they break down the hemoglobin,

145
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:53,380
and it's broken down as follows.

146
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:56,840
The globin, which is made up of amino acids, it's a simple protein,

147
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:58,860
is broken down into amino acids.

148
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:05,300
The heme, which is composed of iron and protoporphyrin, is broken down into iron and protoporphyrin.

149
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,900
The iron is recycled by the body,

150
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:13,800
and the protoporphyrin is subsequently broken down into unconjugated bilirubin.

151
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:19,920
Now, of course, the unconjugated bilirubin will then be bound to the serum albumin

152
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:24,700
and delivered to the liver for conjugation and excretion into the bile.

153
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:27,520
So the unconjugated bilirubin goes into the serum.

154
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:29,280
It is not water-soluble.

155
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:30,440
It is fat-soluble.

156
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:35,500
It will remain within the serum until the liver can take it and conjugate it,

157
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:39,300
and then the liver, once it's conjugated, will put it into the bile.

158
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:46,320
So patients who have extravascular hemolysis will have clinical findings that are based on this paradigm.

159
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:50,840
Of course, they will have anemia because they are destroying red blood cells.

160
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:56,720
They will have splenomegaly because the spleen is consuming those red blood cells,

161
00:08:56,860 --> 00:08:59,320
and this is an example of hypertrophy.

162
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:02,580
In addition, the patients will have jaundice.

163
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:03,920
Why do they have jaundice?

164
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,760
Well, let's explain that.

165
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:12,120
If the patient is producing from the spleen unconjugated bilirubin,

166
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:15,340
that unconjugated bilirubin will go to the liver,

167
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:21,080
and it's in the liver, this being the liver, where it becomes conjugated bilirubin,

168
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:25,020
and the conjugated bilirubin will eventually be dumped into the gallbladder.

169
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:26,380
So that's the paradigm.

170
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:33,860
Now let's pretend that the liver normally has the ability to conjugate about 10 molecules every second.

171
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:39,360
It has the ability to conjugate 10 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin every second.

172
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:44,380
Now let's say that normally, just by the turnover of red blood cells,

173
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:47,760
remember that the spleen is where the destruction of red blood cells occurs,

174
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:51,860
even when blood cells are turning over every 120 days.

175
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:59,180
So let's say that the spleen spits out five molecules of unconjugated bilirubin.

176
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:04,020
That's the normal, and we wouldn't have any problem with jaundice.

177
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:10,980
In the case of extravascular hemolysis, the patient is destroying red blood cells at a much higher rate.

178
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:14,820
And so instead of five molecules of unconjugated bilirubin every second,

179
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:19,760
this may go up to 20 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin every second.

180
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:24,840
Now these 20 molecules are going to override the conjugating ability of the liver,

181
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:31,800
and so therefore what's going to happen is that there's going to be an excess of bilirubin floating around in the blood,

182
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:35,460
unconjugated bilirubin, that is, floating around in the blood.

183
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:39,500
And because there is an excess of unconjugated bilirubin floating around in the blood,

184
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:41,960
the patients will look yellow and develop jaundice.

185
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,560
Again, I've explained that's due to unconjugated bilirubin.

186
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:49,320
At the same time, eventually the liver will conjugate that bilirubin,

187
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:52,580
and when it conjugates that bilirubin, it'll throw it into the bile,

188
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:56,380
and so we're going to get a supersaturation of bilirubin in the bile.

189
00:10:56,380 --> 00:11:02,500
And that supersaturation of bilirubin in the bile is going to increase the risk that the bilirubin would precipitate

190
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:04,660
and form bilirubin gallstones.

191
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:08,640
And we'll talk about gallstones in much more detail when we do your liver lectures.

192
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:11,820
Finally, the patients are going to have marrow hyperplasia.

193
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:13,860
Why would they get marrow hyperplasia?

194
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:18,140
Because the bone marrow is fine, there's no problem in the bone marrow,

195
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,260
and so there will be hyperplasia of the cells of the bone marrow,

196
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:28,040
and the reticulocyte count will increase because the bone marrow will begin to pump out as many baby cells as possible.

197
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:32,560
And so we'll have a corrected reticulocyte count of greater than 3%.

198
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:38,940
In the case of intravascular hemolysis, the red blood cells are being destroyed within the blood vessels.

199
00:11:39,460 --> 00:11:43,260
Now, destruction of the red blood cell within the blood vessel would result in the hemoglobin,

200
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:49,720
which is present within the red cell, to actually be passed directly into the blood.

201
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:54,660
And when hemoglobin gets passed into the blood, the very first response of the body

202
00:11:54,660 --> 00:11:58,760
is to bind it with a scavenger molecule called haptoglobin.

203
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:06,800
Haptoglobin binds hemoglobin and takes it to the spleen to be reprocessed.

204
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:12,440
So that's one way by which the human body can save hemoglobin that might leak out of the red blood cell.

205
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:15,420
And again, why would the human body want to save hemoglobin?

206
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:21,200
Because hemoglobin has iron, and iron is classically very difficult to get a hold of.

207
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:24,820
And so the body tries to save every bit of iron that it can.

208
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:33,400
So the initial stage of loss of hemoglobin in the serum is formation of a hemoglobin-haptoglobin complex,

209
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:39,160
and therefore the patient's haptoglobin levels, the free haptoglobin levels, will actually decrease.

210
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:41,280
So that's one very early change.

211
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:47,840
Now the haptoglobin is minimal within the serum, and it really doesn't do much to save hemoglobin,

212
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:49,680
except in very small amounts.

213
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:55,080
Eventually, the next step will be that hemoglobin will build up in the blood, creating a hemoglobinemia,

214
00:12:55,620 --> 00:13:01,220
and that hemoglobin will leak out into the urine because it's water-soluble, creating a hemoglobinuria.

215
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:05,400
And that allows us to understand the clinical and laboratory findings.

216
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:10,620
So these patients will have hemoglobinemia because hemoglobin will leak out into the blood.

217
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:15,480
They'll have hemoglobinuria because hemoglobin from the blood will leak out into the urine,

218
00:13:15,820 --> 00:13:17,220
and they'll get hemocidinuria.

219
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:18,940
What's hemocidinuria?

220
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:24,520
Well, as the hemoglobin is going through the tubules, let's draw a tubule here.

221
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:27,420
This is the epithelial cell of the tubule.

222
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:31,780
So as, this would be the basement membrane, and this would be the lumen.

223
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:39,500
As hemoglobin is going through this tubule, some of the hemoglobin will be taken up by the renal tubular cells.

224
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:45,160
And as the hemoglobin is taken up by the renal tubular cells, it'll be destroyed, and the iron will be bound,

225
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:48,900
and it'll pile up and bind as hemocidarin.

226
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:52,620
Now as it piles up and binds as hemocidarin, it'll be in the cell,

227
00:13:52,620 --> 00:13:58,620
but eventually these cells, because they're turning over, will actually slough off days after the hemolysis.

228
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:05,980
And so days later, within the urine, patients may have hemocidinuria, and that's actually a high-yield principle.

229
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:10,940
So initially, during the period of hemolysis, they get hemoglobinemia and hemoglobinuria.

230
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:18,200
And then it may be that several days later, the patient will actually have hemocidinuria as these tubular cells are being turned over,

231
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:24,380
and they fall off and then go into the urine and are loaded with the iron from the fact that they consumed hemoglobin.

232
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:31,720
Finally, there will be a decreased serum heptoglobin, and that was the very initial change that occurred with intravascular hemolysis.

233
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:37,300
Again, haptoglobin doesn't really save much of the hemoglobin, but it is an important measure

234
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:42,940
because it tells us that there is some degree of intravascular hemolysis going on.

235
